    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3189/172756404781813907",
    "abstract": "Abstract Statistical analyses are carried out, of the annual mean surface air temperature at occupied stations and automatic weather stations in the Antarctic and Southern and Pacific Oceans. The data are studied in four groupings: coastal Antarctica (excluding the Antarctic Peninsula), inland Antarctica, the Antarctic Peninsula and the Southern Ocean/Pacific Ocean islands. We find that within each of these four groupings the average trend indicates warming. For coastal Antarctica the trend is \u223c0.8\u00b0C(100 a) \u20131 . Inland, the results are less clear, but the mean trend is to a warming of \u223c1.0\u00b0C(100 a) \u20131 . For the Peninsula stations it is \u223c4.4\u00b0C(100 a) \u20131 , and for the ocean stations the average trend is \u223c0.8\u00b0C(100 a) \u20131 . The results indicate a reduction in the warming trend since our last analysis 6 years ago. While the Pinatubo (Philippines) volcanic eruption may have had some influence on this reduction in the warming rate, examination of the interannual variations in the temperature record shows variability has continued high since the recovery from any such effect. There has been a further period of cooler temperatures in coastal and inland Antarctica in that time, yet a warmer period in the Peninsula and ocean islands.",
    "field": "Polar climatology",
    "problem": "Assessing long-term Antarctic temperature change is challenged by high interannual variability and complex regional differences, with recent data indicating a reduction in the overall warming rate and periods of cooling in some areas, complicating trends identified in previous studies.",
    "method": "This paper assesses temperature changes by statistically analyzing annual mean surface air temperature data from stations grouped into four regions\u2014coastal, inland, Peninsula, and ocean\u2014to determine linear trends and their significance for the period 1949\u20132002, identifying patterns of warming and cooling.",
    "task": "The task is to statistically analyze annual mean surface air temperature data from stations in the Antarctic and Southern Ocean between 1949 and 2002 to assess and compare long-term warming trends across four distinct geographical groupings.",
